\pard\plain\s3\ql\sb240\sa120\keepn\f0\b\fs32\sl240\slmult1 \sb240 \fi0 2  Synopsis\par
\pard\plain\s0\qj\widctlpar\f0\fs20\sl240\slmult1 \sb60 \fi0 Using methane as a novel reductant, experimental and modeling efforts related to catalytic deoxygenation kinetics of sustainable bio-oils upgrading are reported. \par
\pard\plain\s3\ql\sb240\sa120\keepn\f0\b\fs32\sl240\slmult1 \sb240 \fi0 3  Abstract\par
\pard\plain\s0\qj\widctlpar\f0\fs20\sl240\slmult1 \sb60 \fi0 Using H{{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ \\s\\do5({\fs16 2})}}{\fldrslt }}
} as reductant, catalytic hydrodeoxygenation (HDO) is typically used for upgrading bio-oils, generally produced from thermal degradation of lignin. In our recent prior work, methane was used to deoxygenate guaiacol over Pt-Bi catalyst and was found to exhibit as good deoxygenation performance as hydrogen. In the present work, using methane as reductant, detailed reaction pathways and kinetics of guaiacol deoxygenation are studied using differential and integral operating conditions. Kinetic parameters including rate constants and activation energies are determined for each individual reaction step. The model predicted values match experimental data well. Results from the present work are discussed and compared with the literature values. The present work provides a practical novel approach for bio-oil upgrading using methane as reductant instead of hydrogen.\par
\column
\pard\plain\s3\ql\sb240\sa120\keepn\f0\b\fs32\sl240\slmult1 \sb240 \fi0 4  Introduction\par
\pard\plain\s0\qj\widctlpar\f0\fs20\sl240\slmult1 \sb60 \fi0 Energy security and environmental concerns are drivers for utilization of biomass to produce bio-fuels. The vast quantity of biomass available in the United States has the potential to replace significant amounts of fuels that are currently derived from petroleum sources. Bio-oil, generally derived from fast pyrolysis of biomass, is one such candidate fuel. 
[{\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BIB_Tuck2012 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{{1}{2012}{{Tuck et\~al.}}{{Tuck, P{\'e9}rez, Horv{\'e1}th, Sheldon, and Poliakoff}}}}}
] Its production process, however, remains under investigation, owing primarily to its high oxygen content, which may cause instability, poor combustion performance and low heating value. 
[{\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BIB_Nigam2011 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{{2}{2011}{{Nigam and Singh}}{{}}}}}, {\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BIB_Elliott2009 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{{3}{2009}{{Elliott and Hart}}{{}}}}}, {\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BIB_Mortensen2011 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{{4}{2011}{{Mortensen et\~al.}}{{Mortensen, Grunwaldt, Jensen, Knudsen, and Jensen}}}}}
] Too high levels of oxygen make pyrolysis oils distinctly different from petroleum fuels. Oxygen removal from pyrolysis bio-oils, thus, is typically followed as an upgrading process to utilize these bio-oils as transportation fuels.
[{\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BIB_Xiu2012 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{{5}{2012}{{Xiu and Shahbazi}}{{}}}}}, {\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BIB_Elliott2015 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{{6}{2015}{{Elliott et\~al.}}{{Elliott, Wang, Rover, Whitmer, Smith, and Brown}}}}}
]\par
\pard\plain\s0\qj\widctlpar\f0\fs20\sl240\slmult1 \fi0 Since the pyrolysis bio-oil is always a mixture of more than 400 chemical species, and different biomass sources result in different bio-oil compositions, model compounds such as phenol, guaiacol (2-methoxyphenol) and benzylphenyl ether
[{\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BIB_Guvenatam2014 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{{7}{2014}{{Guvenatam et\~al.}}{{Guvenatam, Kursun, Heeres, Pidko, and Hensen}}}}}
], are typically selected to represent bio-oils for more fundamental studies
[{\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BIB_Bridgwater2012 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{{8}{2012}{{Bridgwater}}{{}}}}}
] Among these model compounds, guaiacol is attractive because it contains two common oxygenated groups in bio-oils: hydroxyl and methoxyl. Catalytic hydrodeoxygenation (HDO), as a common bio-oil upgrading approach, refers to oxygen removal by the use of H{{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ \\s\\do5({\fs16 2})}}{\fldrslt }}
} molecules over heterogeneous catalysts. Using Pt, Pd, Co, etc. as primary metals, 
[{\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BIB_Wildschut2009 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{{9}{2009}{{Wildschut et\~al.}}{{Wildschut, Mahfud, Venderbosch, and Heeres}}}}}
] Mo, S, etc. as promoters
[{\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BIB_Wangt2011 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{{10}{2011}{{Wang et\~al.}}{{Wang, Yang, Luo, Peng, and Wang}}}}}
], and activated carbon (AC), Al{{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ \\s\\do5({\fs16 2})}}{\fldrslt }}
}O{{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ \\s\\do5({\fs16 3})}}{\fldrslt }}
}, ZrO{{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ \\s\\do5({\fs16 2})}}{\fldrslt }}
}, SiO{{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ \\s\\do5({\fs16 2})}}{\fldrslt }}
}, etc. as catalytst supports,
[{\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BIB_Gutierrez2009 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{{11}{2009}{{Gutierrez et\~al.}}{{Gutierrez, Kaila, Honkela, Slioor, and Krause}}}}}, {\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BIB_Saidi2014 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{{12}{2014}{{Saidi et\~al.}}{{Saidi, Samimi, Karimipourfard, Nimmanwudipong, Gates, and Rahimpour}}}}}, {\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BIB_Wu2013 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{{13}{2013}{{Wu et\~al.}}{{Wu, Lai, Lin, Wan, Lee, and Chang}}}}}
] various experimental and theoretical studies have been reported for guaiacol catalytic HDO.
[{\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BIB_Zhao2011 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{{14}{2011}{{Zhao et\~al.}}{{Zhao, Li, Bui, and Oyama}}}}}, {\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BIB_Bui2011 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{{15}{2011}{{Bui et\~al.}}{{Bui, Laurenti, Afanasiev, and Geantet}}}}}, {\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BIB_Zhang2013 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{{16}{2013}{{Zhang et\~al.}}{{Zhang, Wang, Ma, Zhang, Yu, and Liu}}}}}, {\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BIB_Mochizuki2014 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{{17}{2014}{{Mochizuki et\~al.}}{{Mochizuki, Chen, Toba, and Yoshimura}}}}}, {\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BIB_Olcese2012 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{{18}{2012}{{Olcese et\~al.}}{{Olcese, Bettahar, Petitjean, Malaman, Giovanella, and Dufour}}}}}, {\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BIB_Badawi2011 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{{19}{2011}{{Badawi et\~al.}}{{Badawi, Paul, Cristol, and Payen}}}}}, {\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BIB_Lee2015 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{{20}{2015}{{Lee et\~al.}}{{Lee, Gu, Mullen, Boateng, and Vlachos}}}}}
]\par
\pard\plain\s0\qj\widctlpar\f0\fs20\sl240\slmult1 \fi0 Although H{{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ \\s\\do5({\fs16 2})}}{\fldrslt }}
} is an ideal reductant for oxygen removal from pyrolysis oils, its use for HDO, however, leads to possible economic concerns owing to high cost for H{{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ \\s\\do5({\fs16 2})}}{\fldrslt }}
} production and transportation. For this reason, we have recently reported a novel bio-oil upgrading approach
[{\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BIB_Xiao2015 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{{21}{2015}{{Xiao and Varma}}{{}}}}}
], using CH{{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ \\s\\do5({\fs16 4})}}{\fldrslt }}
} instead of H{{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ \\s\\do5({\fs16 2})}}{\fldrslt }}
} as reductant and Pt-Bi/AC as catalyst, to deoxygenate guaiacol efficiently. It was found that using the mono-metallic Pt/AC catalyst, CH{{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ \\s\\do5({\fs16 4})}}{\fldrslt }}
} exhibited as good deoxygenation performance as H{{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ \\s\\do5({\fs16 2})}}{\fldrslt }}
} in terms of guaiacol conversion and product distribution, but the catalyst deactivated rapidly. With addition of bismuth as promoter, the lifetime of the bi-metallic Pt-Bi/AC was extended significantly without loss of activity, as compared to the Pt/AC catalyst. In our other prior works
[{\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BIB_Gao2014 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{{22}{2014}{{Gao et\~al.}}{{Gao, Schweitzer, Hwang, and Varma}}}}}, {\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BIB_Gao2015 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{{23}{2015}{{Gao et\~al.}}{{Gao, Xiao, and Varma}}}}}
], using Pt/AC and H{{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ \\s\\do5({\fs16 2})}}{\fldrslt }}
}, reaction pathways and detailed kinetics of guaiacol deoxygenation process were reported.\par
\pard\plain\s0\qj\widctlpar\f0\fs20\sl240\slmult1 \fi0 On the basis of these prior studies
[{\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BIB_Xiao2015 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{{21}{2015}{{Xiao and Varma}}{{}}}}}, {\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BIB_Gao2014 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{{22}{2014}{{Gao et\~al.}}{{Gao, Schweitzer, Hwang, and Varma}}}}}, {\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BIB_Gao2015 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{{23}{2015}{{Gao et\~al.}}{{Gao, Xiao, and Varma}}}}}
], in the present work, both experimental and modeling investigations were conducted, to reveal the reaction pathways and kinetics of guaiacol deoxygenation using methane as the reductant, Kinetics measurements in both differential and integral reactors were carried out, and rate constants and activation energy values were determined for the individual reaction steps. These results are discussed and compared with our prior studies as well as other related works.\par
\pard\plain\s3\ql\sb240\sa120\keepn\f0\b\fs32\sl240\slmult1 \sb240 \fi0 5  Experimental\par
\pard\plain\s4\ql\sb240\sa120\keepn\f0\b\fs32\sl240\slmult1 \sb180 \fi0 5.1  Materials\par
\pard\plain\s0\qj\widctlpar\f0\fs20\sl240\slmult1 \sb60 \fi0 The 5%Pt-1%Bi/C catalysts were prepared following the procedure described in our prior works.
[{\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BIB_Hu2010 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{{24}{2010}{{Hu et\~al.}}{{Hu, Knight, Lowry, and Varma}}}}}, {\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BIB_Hu2011 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{{25}{2011}{{Hu et\~al.}}{{Hu, Lowry, and Varma}}}}}, {\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BIB_Xiao2015 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{{21}{2015}{{Xiao and Varma}}{{}}}}}
] The metallic precursors were chloroplatinic acid hydrate (99.9% metal basis) and bismuth chloride (99.999%), both from Sigma Aldrich. The 20-120 mesh AC supports were from Norit Americas Inc. Briefly, Pt and Bi were loaded sequentially using the wet impregnation method. First, the Pt and Bi precursors were dissolved in 1.2 mol/L HCl and then added dropwise to the well-stirred AC slurry, with stirring continued for at least 8 hrs at room temperature. The mixture was then rinsed and dried in air at 100 {{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ \\s\\up5({\fs16 {\u8728*}})}}{\fldrslt }}
}C before use. Guaiacol (>98.0%) and all other calibration compounds (catechol, phenol and cyclopentanone) were purchased from Sigma-Aldrich. GUA, CAT, PHE and CYC are used for abbreviations of Guaiacol, catechol, phenol and cyclopentanone, respectively in the later sections. Ultra high purity grade gases (99.98% oxygen, 99.999% argon, 99.98% helium and 99.999% hydrogen) were purchased from Indiana Oxygen.\par
\pard\plain\s0\qj\widctlpar\f0\fs20\sl240\slmult1 \fi0 The 0.5%Pt/Al{{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ \\s\\do5({\fs16 2})}}{\fldrslt }}
}O{{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ \\s\\do5({\fs16 3})}}{\fldrslt }}
} (metal dispersion=31 {{\u177*}} 0.5%) standard (for chemisorption calibration) was from Micromeritics.\par
\pard\plain\s4\ql\sb240\sa120\keepn\f0\b\fs32\sl240\slmult1 \sb120 \fi0 5.2  Kinetics Measurements\par
\pard\plain\s0\qj\widctlpar\f0\fs20\sl240\slmult1 \sb60 \fi0 The kinetics measurements were conducted in a fixed-bed reactor setup, described in our prior work. 
[{\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BIB_Gao2014 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{{22}{2014}{{Gao et\~al.}}{{Gao, Schweitzer, Hwang, and Varma}}}}}, {\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BIB_Gao2015 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{{23}{2015}{{Gao et\~al.}}{{Gao, Xiao, and Varma}}}}}
] Prior to reaction, the packed catalyst was activated at 400 {{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ \\s\\up5({\fs16 {\u8728*}})}}{\fldrslt }}
}C and 1 atm for 4 hours under a gas mixture flow (H{{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ \\s\\do5({\fs16 2})}}{\fldrslt }}
}:N{{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ \\s\\do5({\fs16 2})}}{\fldrslt }}
} = 1:2). The reactor was then purged using N{{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ \\s\\do5({\fs16 2})}}{\fldrslt }}
} for 30 min. The standard reactor operating conditions were: 400 {{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ \\s\\up5({\fs16 {\u8728*}})}}{\fldrslt }}
}C, 1 atm, 0.02-0.05 g catalyst for differential reactions (conversions typically < 6-8%) and 0.3-0.5 g catalyst for integral reactions (conversions typically > 20%), total gas (CH{{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ \\s\\do5({\fs16 4})}}{\fldrslt }}
}:N{{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ \\s\\do5({\fs16 2})}}{\fldrslt }}
} = 1:1) flow rate 100 mL/min, and guaiacol feed rate 0.025 mL/min (liquid, at room temperature).\par
\pard\plain\s0\qj\widctlpar\f0\fs20\sl240\slmult1 \fi0 The feed flow rates correspond to a molar ratio of 10 between CH{{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ \\s\\do5({\fs16 4})}}{\fldrslt }}
} and guaiacol. Blank tests of AC support with no metal loading were conducted under the standard reaction conditions, and guaiacol conversion was less than 1%. Without guaiacol, methane exhibited about 5% conversion, generating H{{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ \\s\\do5({\fs16 2})}}{\fldrslt }}
} and trace C{{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ \\s\\do5({\fs16 2})}}{\fldrslt }}
}H{{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ \\s\\do5({\fs16 6})}}{\fldrslt }}
} on Pt-Bi catalyst under the standard conditions. All experiments have carbon mass balances of 92 {{\u177*}} 2%, similar to the literature. 
[{\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BIB_Chang2013 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{{26}{2013}{{Chang et\~al.}}{{Chang, Danuthai, Dewiyanti, Wang, and Borgna}}}}}, {\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BIB_Jongerius2013 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{{27}{2013}{{Jongerius et\~al.}}{{Jongerius, Gosselink, Dijkstra, Bitter, Bruijnincx, and Weckhuysen}}}}}
] Possible factors affecting mass balance include liquid hold-up in various locations in the system, particularly the condenser, and coke deposit on the catalyst.\par
\pard\plain\s4\ql\sb240\sa120\keepn\f0\b\fs32\sl240\slmult1 \sb120 \fi0 5.3  Product Analysis\par
\pard\plain\s0\qj\widctlpar\f0\fs20\sl240\slmult1 \sb60 \fi0 As in our prior works, GC (Agilent GC6890) with flame ionization detector (FID), equipped with a DB-1701 column (30 m {{\u215*}} 0.25 mm) was used for quantitative analysis of the liquid products. 
[{\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BIB_Gao2014 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{{22}{2014}{{Gao et\~al.}}{{Gao, Schweitzer, Hwang, and Varma}}}}}, {\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BIB_Gao2015 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{{23}{2015}{{Gao et\~al.}}{{Gao, Xiao, and Varma}}}}}, {\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BIB_Xiao2015 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{{21}{2015}{{Xiao and Varma}}{{}}}}}
] The gaseous effluent was analyzed using a Micro GC (Agilent 3000A Micro GC) equipped with two columns (Column A, MolSieve 5 A, 10 m {{\u215*}} 0.32 mm; Column B: Plot U, 8 m {{\u215*}} 0.32 mm) and two thermal conductivity detectors (TCD). For the reaction experiments, good repeatability generally within less than 2% deviation was achieved for all quantitative analysis.\par
\pard\plain\s3\ql\sb240\sa120\keepn\f0\b\fs32\sl240\slmult1 \sb240 \fi0 6  Results and Discussion\par
\pard\plain\s4\ql\sb240\sa120\keepn\f0\b\fs32\sl240\slmult1 \sb180 \fi0 6.1  Proposed Reaction Pathways\par
\pard\plain\s0\qj\widctlpar\f0\fs20\sl240\slmult1 \sb60 \fi0 In our prior work, when Pt/AC as catalyst and H{{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ \\s\\do5({\fs16 2})}}{\fldrslt }}
} as reductant were used for guaiacol deoxygenation, reactions pathways, as shown in Figure 1a, were proposed and confirmed by experimental observations
[{\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BIB_Gao2014 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{{22}{2014}{{Gao et\~al.}}{{Gao, Schweitzer, Hwang, and Varma}}}}}
], kinetic modeling and DFT calculations.
[{\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BIB_Gao2015 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{{23}{2015}{{Gao et\~al.}}{{Gao, Xiao, and Varma}}}}}
] Similar reactions pathways, as shown in Figure 1b, apply in the present work since the same product species, including GUA, CAT, PHE and CYC, and similar distribution are found when Pt-Bi/AC as catalyst and CH{{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ \\s\\do5({\fs16 4})}}{\fldrslt }}
} as reductant are used. Both Figure 1a and Figure 1b describe reaction pathways containing five individual steps, denoted as R1-R5, producing catechol, phenol and cyclopentanone as liquid products, while different gaseous by-products arise from H{{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ \\s\\do5({\fs16 2})}}{\fldrslt }}
} and CH{{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ \\s\\do5({\fs16 4})}}{\fldrslt }}
}, respectively. Experimentally, no significant conversion was found when phenol and cyclopentanone were used as reactants in feed, indicating no additional reactions beyond steps R1-R5. \par
