\pard\plain\s9\qc\sb120\sa120\keep\widctlpar\f0\sl240\slmult1 \fi0 Figure 3. Mass transfer limitation tests for a) internal diffusion effect and b) external diffusion effect \par
}\pard\plain\s0\qj\widctlpar\f0\fs20\sl240\slmult1 \fi0 Figure 3a was obtained by varying packed catalyst particle diameter ({{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ {\i d}\\s\\do5({\fs16 {\i p}})}}{\fldrslt }}
}) in the range 100 micron < {{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ {\i d}\\s\\do5({\fs16 {\i p}})}}{\fldrslt }}
} < 1000 micron, while keeping other operating conditions unchanged. Figure 3b was obtained by varying feed flow rates, while maintaining contact time (W/F) and other operating conditions constant. Figure 3a shows that when catalyst particle diameter ({{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ {\i d}\\s\\do5({\fs16 {\i p}})}}{\fldrslt }}
}) is larger than 250 micron, corresponding to 60 mesh, internal diffusion effects are discerned, while when ({{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ {\i d}\\s\\do5({\fs16 {\i p}})}}{\fldrslt }}
}) < 150 micron, corresponding to 100 mesh, no significant internal effects are found. As shown in Figure 3b, when feed flow rate is smaller than 1.2 mL/min, there is an obvious external mass transfer effect, while no such effect is observed when feed flow rate is larger than 1.5 mL/min. Thus, ({{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ {\i d}\\s\\do5({\fs16 {\i p}})}}{\fldrslt }}
}) < 150 micron and feed flow rate larger than 1.5 mL/min were used in the kinetics measurement experiments. Further, the criteria described by Weisz and Prater 
[{\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BIB_Weisz1954 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{{29}{1954}{{Weisz and Prater}}{{}}}}}
] and Mears 
[{\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BIB_Mears1971 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{{30}{1971}{{Mears}}{{}}}}}
] were both satisfied to verify the absence of mass and heat transfer effects in all experiments.\par
