}\pard\plain\s0\qj\widctlpar\f0\fs20\sl240\slmult1 \fi0 Using different feed partial pressures of guaiacol or catechol, reaction rates were measured and plotted with respect to average bed reactant partial pressures, as shown in Figure 4. All three data sets follow linear regressions, indicating first orders for steps R1, R2 and R5. Thus, the corresponding reaction rates at 400 {{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ \\s\\up5({\fs16 {\u8728*}})}}{\fldrslt }}
}C are described by in Eqs. ({\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BMEq11 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{10}}} -\~{\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BMEq13 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{12}}}), where the regressed values of {{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ {\i k}\\s\\do5({\fs16 1})}}{\fldrslt }}
}, {{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ {\i k}\\s\\do5({\fs16 2})}}{\fldrslt }}
} and {{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ {\i k}\\s\\do5({\fs16 5})}}{\fldrslt }}
} are listed in Table 1. The reaction rate regressions of R1, R2 and R5 at other temperatures are also essentially linear, as discussed later in the section Activation Energies and Temperature Effect.\par
