\pard\plain\s0\qj\widctlpar\f0\fs20\sl240\slmult1 \fi0 The {{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ {\i w}\\s\\do5({\fs16 {\i i}})}}{\fldrslt }}
} in Eq.\~({\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BMEq19 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{18}}}) is the weighting function. In principle, any positive weighting function can be used. For single reactions, the appropriate {{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ {\i w}\\s\\do5({\fs16 {\i i}})}}{\fldrslt }}
} have been reported by de Levie 
[{\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BIB_Levie1986 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{{35}{1986}{{de\~Levie}}{{}}}}}
], but are not available for simultaneous multi-reaction network, as in the present case. Following de Levie, {{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ {\i w}\\s\\do5({\fs16 {\i i}})=1}}{\fldrslt }}
}, {{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ {\i F}\\s\\up({\fs16 2,{\i i}})}}{\fldrslt }}
}, {{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ {\i F}\\s\\up({\fs16 4,{\i i}})}}{\fldrslt }}
} and {{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ {\i F}\\s\\up({\fs16 6,{\i i}})}}{\fldrslt }}
} are appropriate for single reactions with orders 0, 1, 2 and 3, respectively. 
[{\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BIB_Levie1986 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{{35}{1986}{{de\~Levie}}{{}}}}}
]\par
\pard\plain\s0\qj\widctlpar\f0\fs20\sl240\slmult1 \fi0 Since the differential reaction analysis yielded first-order for steps R1, R2 and R5, we used {{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ {\i w}\\s\\do5({\fs16 {\i i}})={\i F}\\s\\up({\fs16 2,{\i i}})}}{\fldrslt }}
} as the starting weighting functions. A sequential optimization strategy was then employed where only 2 ({{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ {\i k}\\s\\do5({\fs16 3})}}{\fldrslt }}
} and {{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ {\i k}\\s\\do5({\fs16 4})}}{\fldrslt }}
}) or 4 ({{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ {\i k}\\s\\do5({\fs16 3})}}{\fldrslt }}
}, {{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ {\i k}\\s\\do5({\fs16 4})}}{\fldrslt }}
}, {{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ {\i n}\\s\\do5({\fs16 3})}}{\fldrslt }}
} and {{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ {\i n}\\s\\do5({\fs16 4})}}{\fldrslt }}
}) variables out of 10 were kept flexible at the initial stage. Using these results as initial guesses, all 10 variables were allowed to vary in the final stage and the corresponding optimized parameters are shown in Table 2. Since the reaction orders {{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ {\i n}\\s\\do5({\fs16 3})}}{\fldrslt }}
} and {{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ {\i n}\\s\\do5({\fs16 4})}}{\fldrslt }}
} are 2, {{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ {\i w}\\s\\do5({\fs16 {\i i}})={\i F}\\s\\up({\fs16 4,{\i i}})}}{\fldrslt }}
} was also tested, and converged to the same {{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ {\i n}\\s\\do5({\fs16 {\i i}})}}{\fldrslt }}
} as for {{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ {\i w}\\s\\do5({\fs16 {\i i}})={\i F}\\s\\up({\fs16 2,{\i i}})}}{\fldrslt }}
} and essentially the same {{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ {\i k}\\s\\do5({\fs16 {\i i}})}}{\fldrslt }}
} values as well. Thus it appears that {{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ {\i w}\\s\\do5({\fs16 {\i i}})={\i F}\\s\\up({\fs16 2,{\i i}})}}{\fldrslt }}
} is an appropriate choice for the weighting function.\par
