}\pard\plain\s0\qj\widctlpar\f0\fs20\sl240\slmult1 \fi0 The activation energy of step R1 was reported to be 71.2, 58.7 and 89.1 {{\i k}{\i J}/{\i m}{\i o}{\i l}} for Co{\u8722?}Mo, Ni{\u8722?}Mo and Ni{\u8722?}Cu catalysts, respectively, 
[{\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BIB_Laurent1994 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{{36}{1994}{{Laurent and Delmon}}{{}}}}}, {\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BIB_Bykova2013 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{{37}{2013}{{Bykova et\~al.}}{{Bykova, Zavarukhin, Trusov, and Yakovlev}}}}}
] when H{{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ \\s\\do5({\fs16 2})}}{\fldrslt }}
} was used as reductant. There is no data available for the CH{{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ \\s\\do5({\fs16 4})}}{\fldrslt }}
} case in the literature. Using Pt-based catalysts, our activation energies of step R1 are 146 and 126 {{\i k}{\i J}/{\i m}{\i o}{\i l}} for the CH{{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ \\s\\do5({\fs16 4})}}{\fldrslt }}
} and H{{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ \\s\\do5({\fs16 2})}}{\fldrslt }}
} cases, respectively, both higher than literature data. The difference likely owes to the catalyst itself, including Pt metal nature, Pt particle size, support effect and Bi promoter addition 
[{\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BIB_Deepa2014 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{{38}{2014}{{Deepa and Dhepe}}{{}}}}}, {\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BIB_Xiao2015 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{{21}{2015}{{Xiao and Varma}}{{}}}}}
]. Table 3 also shows that activation energies for the CH{{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ \\s\\do5({\fs16 4})}}{\fldrslt }}
} case are always 10-35 {{\i k}{\i J}/{\i m}{\i o}{\i l}} higher (except R3) than those for the H{{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ \\s\\do5({\fs16 2})}}{\fldrslt }}
} case. This is an explanation for the fact that, to achieve a similar guaiacol conversion at 300 {{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ \\s\\up5({\fs16 {\u8728*}})}}{\fldrslt }}
}C for the H{{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ \\s\\do5({\fs16 2})}}{\fldrslt }}
} case, 400 {{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ \\s\\up5({\fs16 {\u8728*}})}}{\fldrslt }}
}C is needed for the CH{{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ \\s\\do5({\fs16 4})}}{\fldrslt }}
} case as shown in our prior work.
[{\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BIB_Xiao2015 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{{21}{2015}{{Xiao and Varma}}{{}}}}}
]. For step R3, activation energy of the H{{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ \\s\\do5({\fs16 2})}}{\fldrslt }}
} case is only 93 {{\i k}{\i J}/{\i m}{\i o}{\i l}}, while it is 166 {{\i k}{\i J}/{\i m}{\i o}{\i l}} for the CH{{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ \\s\\do5({\fs16 4})}}{\fldrslt }}
} case. This is likely because H{{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ \\s\\do5({\fs16 2})}}{\fldrslt }}
} promotes step R3 by forming a more stable by-product CH{{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ \\s\\do5({\fs16 3})}}{\fldrslt }}
}OH than C{{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ \\s\\do5({\fs16 2})}}{\fldrslt }}
}H{{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ \\s\\do5({\fs16 6})}}{\fldrslt }}
}, the by-product from the CH{{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ \\s\\do5({\fs16 4})}}{\fldrslt }}
} case.\par
\pard\plain\s0\qj\widctlpar\f0\fs20\sl240\slmult1 \fi0 In the present work, kinetics of guaiacol deoxygenation using methane as reductant over Pt-Bi/AC catalyst is studied by both experimental and modeling approaches. It is shown that the liquid phase products remain the same as for the case using hydrogen as reductant.
[{\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BIB_Gao2015 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{{23}{2015}{{Gao et\~al.}}{{Gao, Xiao, and Varma}}}}}
] The activation energies and other kinetic parameters are obtained based on data fit. The experimental data matches model predictions well. Modeling results are discussed, analyzed and compared with literature reports. The present work provides a new practical approach for bio-oil upgrading using methane as reductant instead of hydrogen.\par
